WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.500
Could an enzyme step fit inside 40 microseconds?

2
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.660
First, you need to know what that number measures.

3
00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:16.633
In 1979, Corey and Hudspeth moved the hair bundles of a bullfrog sacculus, in a

4
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:17.633
dish.

5
00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:20.687
They recorded the transepithelial microphonic.

6
00:00:21.447 --> 00:00:25.147
That is the summed electrical response across the whole sheet of cells.

7
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.120
The epithelium's own electrical time constant slows that signal.

8
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.640
So the recorded delay is longer than the true one.

9
00:00:34.583 --> 00:00:39.843
After correcting for it, the delay was 40 microseconds, at 22 degrees Celsius.

10
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:46.927
Predict. Suppose an enzyme had to make a chemical messenger between the bundle and

11
00:00:46.927 --> 00:00:49.787
the channel. Could the response begin that fast?

12
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.440
Only with great difficulty.

13
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.380
Over a gap of nanometres, free diffusion can be fast.

14
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.160
But that alone does not say how soon the messenger finds and binds a channel.

15
00:01:07.183 --> 00:01:08.583
Making it is tight too.

16
00:01:09.463 --> 00:01:15.143
In a simple constant-rate model, one enzyme at 4,000 turnovers a second makes, on

17
00:01:15.143 --> 00:01:17.543
average, only 0.16 molecule.

18
00:01:18.483 --> 00:01:21.463
That is about a 15 percent chance of making even one.

19
00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:24.227
Many enzymes could make more.

20
00:01:25.027 --> 00:01:29.887
To test a messenger mechanism, we would need its enzyme numbers, activation and

21
00:01:29.887 --> 00:01:34.007
binding rates, geometry, and the messenger level that changes the current.

22
00:01:34.847 --> 00:01:38.947
This is a model check, not a result of the 1979 experiment.

23
00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:41.690
Now, what the number is not.

24
00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.630
It is not the opening time of one channel.

25
00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:46.790
And it is not a mouse number.

26
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.440
Channel speed was measured directly later.

27
00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:56.340
Ricci and colleagues found time constants as short as about 50 microseconds, in

28
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.340
turtle hair cells.

29
00:01:58.183 --> 00:02:01.863
So in turtle hair cells, channels do open on this time scale.

30
00:02:02.743 --> 00:02:05.843
The 1979 number simply measured something else.

31
00:02:06.833 --> 00:02:09.033
Four kinds of number are often merged.

32
00:02:09.853 --> 00:02:13.813
A tissue response latency is the delay of a whole population of cells.

33
00:02:14.883 --> 00:02:19.883
A channel activation time constant is how fast current rises in a patch or cell.

34
00:02:20.866 --> 00:02:24.326
A molecular gain counts output events per input event.

35
00:02:25.266 --> 00:02:30.426
And a behavioural latency runs from stimulus to movement, through conduction, spinal

36
00:02:30.426 --> 00:02:31.426
circuits and muscle.

37
00:02:32.499 --> 00:02:35.299
Speed can be compatible with a candidate channel.

38
00:02:36.079 --> 00:02:38.139
But speed alone does not identify it.

39
00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:43.193
So before you compare two speeds, ask whether they are the same kind of number.
